Hexenwail - Compiling from source
=================================

Hexenwail uses CMake and builds on Linux and Windows.
The recommended way to build on NixOS/Nix is with the provided flake.


Building with Nix (recommended):
================================

    nix build

This produces the binary in result/bin/.


Building with CMake:
====================

Prerequisites:
  - CMake 3.20+
  - A C compiler with C11 support (GCC 10+, Clang 12+)
  - Rust 1.82 or newer (cargo and rustc; engine/rust/rust-toolchain.toml
    names the version CI uses).  Not optional: part of the engine is
    written in Rust and linked into every build as a static library.  CMake
    finds cargo on PATH, or pass -DCARGO_EXECUTABLE=/path/to/cargo.
  - SDL3 development libraries
  - OpenGL 4.3 capable driver

Optional libraries for music playback:
  - libogg and libvorbis (or libtremor) for OGG Vorbis
  - libmad or libmpg123 for MP3
  - libogg, opus, and opusfile for Opus
  - libFLAC for FLAC
  - libxmp or libmikmod for tracker music (MOD/S3M/XM/IT)

Build steps:

    mkdir build && cd build
    cmake ..
    make

The binary will be named glhexen2 (Linux) or glh2.exe (Windows).


Building against OpenGL ES 3.0 instead of desktop GL:
=====================================================

    cmake -DUSE_GLES=ON ..

The renderer has a second tier that targets GL ES 3.0 rather than GL 4.3: GL
entry points resolved at link time instead of by dlsym, "#version 300 es"
shaders, and no SSBO, compute, buffer_storage, fence sync, timer query or Hi-Z
cull.  The Emscripten/WebGL2 build implies it automatically; -DUSE_GLES=ON is
how you reach it on the desktop.

You need glesv2.pc and the GLES3/ headers, both of which libglvnd already
supplies wherever desktop GL is installed.  The resulting binary links
libGLESv2 rather than libGL and requests an ES context from SDL.

This is the cheap way to exercise the tier without a browser -- it runs against
Mesa, including llvmpipe under SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen -- and it is the same
tier ANGLE would serve on macOS/Metal.


Cross-compiling for Windows:
============================

Use MinGW-w64 with CMake:

    mkdir build-win && cd build-win
    cmake -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=../engine/cmake/toolchain-mingw64.cmake ../engine
    make

The Rust half is built for x86_64-pc-windows-gnu, which CMake derives from the
MinGW toolchain, so that target's standard library must be installed:

    rustup target add x86_64-pc-windows-gnu

`nix build .#win64` provides a cross rustc that already carries it.  Any other
cross build has to name its triple: -DRUST_TARGET=<rust target triple>.


Building the WebAssembly client:
================================

See docs/PWA.md.  Besides Emscripten it needs the wasm32-unknown-emscripten
Rust target (`rustup target add wasm32-unknown-emscripten`); `nix develop
.#wasm` provides both.


